        "There is a last sleep, twenty minutes, a chair that records posture, a clock that agrees with no other clock. You take it because Voss told you to and because Zero told you advertisements can lie and because your body has begun to collect debts. The dream is brief and specific: Kane in a lift, Radek in shirts, Voss dumping coffee, Ellis drowning on a form, you choosing a curb that never ends.\n\nYou wake with your name in your mouth and a tailor's pin still in the cloth. Lyle is there with water. You drink. You believe her because you have to believe someone and she is the only one who uses the word heresy as if it were a tool. The pin pricks when you shift. Pain that small is useful. It keeps the dream from climbing back into the cloth.",
        "A distant thump — leftover war or early theater, the distinction melted. Your phone, one more time, Kane: Stay billable. Voss: Ugly is nearby. Zero: The ball never lands. You can put a ball on the floor yourself. That is sabotage. That is also play. I am not supposed to encourage play. I am doing it anyway. Come back after. I paused the park for you.\n\nYou stand. The cloth lies. The flinch holds or it doesn't. The burn sits in the second heart. The street has finished the first lesson of the night: cars are religions, fires are invoices, Kane appears when a file walks. The second lesson is waiting in a room with a client and a light that will not apologize. You walk toward it because the campaign does not offer a plate called away. The chair keeps your posture in a file you will never read. You leave it a little crooked on purpose. Crooked is a hitch. Hitches survive theaters.",
